-- title:
--   Level-one node integers equal the localised plane model
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, a field $k$ of characteristic $q$ which is algebraically closed, and a ring homomorphism $red : A \to k$; fix modular polynomial data `data` for $q$ satisfying the Kronecker congruence $\overline{\Phi} = (Y^q - X)(Y - X^q)$ modulo $q$, integrality hypotheses $h\alpha_1$, $h\beta_1$ for the two degeneracy embeddings of level $1$ into level $q$, a place specialisation $P_1$ for these data at level $1$, and a prolongation tuple $R$ over $P_1$ which satisfies `IsModel` (the two divisor laws and the two cusp laws). Let $W$ be a finite set of places of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\ k\ 1$ all of which are supersingular (rational, affine for $j$ and $j_1$, with $j$-value in $\mathrm{ssJSet}\ q\ k$), and assume $R$ satisfies the regularity law for $W$. Let $K \subseteq \overline{\mathbb Q}$ be a finite extension of $\mathbb Q$, let $w \in W$, let $a \in k$ be the value $w(j)$ of the geometric generator $j$ at $w$, and suppose $a$ is the image under $red$ of some element $x$ of $A \cap K$. Then, for every Laurent series $f$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, the following are equivalent: $f$ lies in the subring $\mathrm{modularLocalizedAtPoint}$, that is, there are two-variable polynomials $r, s$ with coefficients in $A \cap K$ such that $s(a, a^q) \neq 0$ after reducing coefficients by $red$ and $f \cdot s(j, j_q) = r(j, j_q)$ in Laurent series at level $1 \cdot q$; and $f$ is the $q$-expansion of an element $g$ of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}(1 \cdot q)$ belonging to $R.\mathrm{nodeIntegersOver}\ K\ w$, i.e. $g$ lies in the valuation subrings $R_1$ and $R_2$ of the tuple, lies in the valuation subring of every place $V$ of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}(1 \cdot q)$ with $P_1.\mathrm{reduceFst}\ V = w$, and has its $q$-expansion in the subfield of Laurent series generated by the constants from $K$ together with $j$ and $j_q$.
--
--   This is the level-one dictionary between the two descriptions of the local ring at a supersingular node of $X_0(q)$: the places-theoretic ring of node integers over $K$ attached to the prolongation tuple, and the localisation at the point $(a, a^q)$ of the plane model $(A \cap K)[j, j_q]$ cut out by the modular equation. It is used in the construction of node packages and component charts with annuli, and in the computation of crossing exponents at level one.
-- source:
